00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:13.450
For the longest while airlines treated pilots
as a renewable resource.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.660
It was long considered one of the best jobs
one could have—to make good money flying

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.230
around the world in comfort—but as aviation
has become more commonplace the job of pilot

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.690
has too become more and more just like any
other job.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.300
While in the 50s the job garnered the same
respect and pay as a doctor, entering the

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.650
industry today earns you not much more than
you’d make at a fast food restaurant.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.450
Before a pilot can even perform their first
take-off with passengers in the back they

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.500
have to get licensed.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.640
Most top airline jobs require or strongly
favor those with a college diploma so for

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.120
those that want to go all the way in the field
they have to start by getting a degree.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.430
The cost of that averages $133,000 in the
US and then every aspiring commercial pilot

00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:00.850
first needs to get a private pilots license
which requires 35 hours of flight-time.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.730
These first bits of flight time, which are
with an instructor, typically cost about $140

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.600
an hour or $4,900 total.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.000
You also have to take classes which have their
own fees so getting a private pilots license

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.790
usually costs about $8,000 all in.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.250
To start actually making money as a pilot,
though, you need 15 additional hours of instruction

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:26.210
for an instrument rating costing $900 and
an additional 215 hours of flight time costing

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.550
$23,500.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.020
There are also tons of other smaller costs
for books and housing and transportation and

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.890
other things which bring the total cost of
pilots training to at least $80,000.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:45.159
Having done all that, having spent $213,000
on education so far, one finally receives

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:50.840
a commercial pilots license but still, at
that point, one can’t work for most airlines.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.810
To get an Airline Transport Pilot License,
the one needed to work for large commercial

00:01:54.810 --> 00:02:00.210
airlines by the likes of Delta or KLM or Cathay
Pacific, one needs, with some exceptions,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.140
1,500 flight hours.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:08.009
That would cost an additional $136,000 in
rental fees alone if one were to pay for the

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:12.190
flight time themselves so pilots normally
let someone else pay for it by working at

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.170
a job that doesn’t require a full ATP license.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:20.900
The most common job used to get from 250 to
1500 hours is as a flight instructor but some

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.940
also work flying skydiving planes, towing
banners, or for airlines flying small single-engine

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.940
planes.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.620
Once that’s all done, after having been
in education continuously for a quarter of

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.730
ones life until the age of 23 and having spent
hundreds of thousands of dollars one can finally

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.880
start flying commercial passenger planes and
earning $30,000 a year.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.960
That’s a typical and even fair starting
salary for a first year first officer and,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.470
considering these individuals often have tens
or hundreds of thousands of dollars of debt,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:55.400
young pilots are often in a tough financial
situation.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.410
Most Americans pilots start their commercial
careers at regional airlines.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:04.130
The largest of these is SkyWest airlines which
is an airline that flies for other airlines.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:08.989
You could be flying any of the three major
American airlines—Delta, United, or American—and

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:11.640
actually be on a Skywest operated flight.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.200
Skywest, along with other regional airlines,
operate all planes for these US airlines with

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.770
under 76 seats.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:22.260
They fly the smallest planes which are actually
more expensive on a per passenger basis to

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.430
operate than the larger planes.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.940
The regional airlines’ primary purpose is
to lower cost.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:32.380
The aviation industry is heavily unionized
so the major airlines would never be allowed

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.700
to bring in pilots at a yearly salary of twenty
or thirty thousand dollars.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.300
Therefore, they contract these regional carriers
to operate the small expensive planes so the

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.520
crew can be employed at a different pay-scale
even if the flights are branded as United

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.200
or Delta or American.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.350
While a first year first officer for American
Airlines makes $88 per flight hour, a first

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.410
year first officer for SkyWest only makes
$37.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:04:00.940
Pilots are only allowed to fly 900 hours per
calendar year in the US which works out to

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.410
75 hours per month or about 17 hours per week.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:09.410
Now, the idea of a 17 hour workweek probably
sounds great but these are only the hours

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.250
in command of an airborne aircraft.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.990
They don’t account for the time it takes
to get to the airport, clear security, brief

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.989
for the flight, inspect and prep the aircraft,
board passengers, deplane passengers, pack

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:22.740
up, file paperwork, and get home or to a hotel.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.680
Pilots are primarily paid based on hours in
the air which is why you tend to see the more

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.050
senior pilots flying the longer routes.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.240
A typical four-day trip for a regional pilot
in the US might see them flying on Monday

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.630
from Minneapolis to Fargo, Fargo to Minneapolis,
Minneapolis to Pittsburgh, then Pittsburgh

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.729
to Boston where they would stay overnight.

00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:43.319
The next day, Tuesday, they would fly from
Boston to Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh to Boston,

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:45.270
and Boston to Nashville.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:50.009
After an overnight they would fly Nashville
to Boston, Boston to Jacksonville, and Jacksonville

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:51.009
to Boston.

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:54.850
Finally, on Thursday, they would fly from
Boston to Norfolk and then Norfolk to Minneapolis.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:05:00.099
Over that four day, twelve leg trip they would
be in the air for just over 19 hours.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:04.789
That regional pilot would have been almost
constantly either working, sleeping, or eating.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:07.969
Compare that to a long-haul pilot flying an
a330.

00:05:07.969 --> 00:05:12.059
They would leave Atlanta on a Monday night,
fly to Amsterdam arriving Tuesday morning,

00:05:12.059 --> 00:05:16.659
and fly back Thursday after having had a full
48 hours free in Amsterdam.

00:05:16.659 --> 00:05:21.009
On that trip the pilot would have accumulated
16 flight hours—not all that different from

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:25.289
the hours that the regional pilot gained after
working constantly for four days.

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:29.360
It’s no wonder why the most senior pilots,
who have the seniority to decide which type

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.559
of plane and routes they fly, almost always
go for the long-haul routes.

00:05:33.559 --> 00:05:37.259
Meanwhile, while the young pilots don’t
get paid much and work tougher schedules,

00:05:37.259 --> 00:05:41.020
the advantage for them of working for these
regional airlines is that they can gain the

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:45.039
experience and hours needed to get a job at
a more desirable airline.

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:49.060
The problem that the airline industry if facing
is that not enough people are willing to put

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.400
themselves through all the years of expensive
training, low pay, and long hours to get to

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.879
the stage where they make good money and fly
the most interesting routes.

00:05:56.879 --> 00:06:01.580
There is a shortage of pilots but it’s hard
to know how bad this shortage is—some say

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:05.580
it’s a myth, others will say it’s already
chronic—but by one estimate the US alone

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.960
will have 1,600 pilot jobs unfilled by 2020.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:14.009
While the problem is only predicted to get
worse, the shortage of pilots is already having

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:15.009
consequences.

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:19.409
Emirates Airlines denies having any pilot
shortage but the reality is that in April

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:25.379
2018 they flew 36 777’s and 10 a380’s
over to Dubai World Central Airport, a less

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:29.419
busy airport than the main Dubai airport,
and left those airplanes there on the ground

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:32.960
for months because there just weren’t enough
pilots to fly them.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.499
The concerning thing is, Emirates pilots are
some of the best paid in the industry.

00:06:37.499 --> 00:06:42.879
Even young Emirates pilots make more than
$200,000 a year and, thanks to local laws,

00:06:42.879 --> 00:06:44.619
that’s tax-free income.

00:06:44.619 --> 00:06:48.589
Emirates has come under criticism for long
work hours, insufficient rest periods, and

00:06:48.589 --> 00:06:52.460
minimal pilot staffing for their longest haul
flights which is likely a contributing factor

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:56.749
to their difficulty in attracting pilots but,
if they’re having trouble recruiting pilots

00:06:56.749 --> 00:07:00.840
while paying so much that’s not a great
sign for the industry.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.819
These high salaries from some airlines are
also a contributing factor to the shortage

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:06.819
in the US and Europe, though.

00:07:06.819 --> 00:07:11.649
The UAE is a small country with two massive
airlines so they rely on recruiting foreign

00:07:11.649 --> 00:07:12.649
pilots.

00:07:12.649 --> 00:07:17.539
China, while not a small country, also doesn’t
have enough local pilots to staff their tremendously

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:19.550
fast growing airline industry.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:25.770
The country’s airlines therefore pay a starting
salary of $312,000 per year to foreign pilots

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.129
and some make up to $500,000.

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:34.129
While working conditions are tough, it’s
still no wonder why 10% of all pilots in China

00:07:34.129 --> 00:07:35.490
are now foreigners.

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:40.090
With more and more pilots heading overseas
to the best paying pilot jobs the US, Europe,

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:44.690
and other western countries are left with
fewer and fewer people to fly their planes.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:48.439
The short-term solution for airlines in the
US has been to fast track pilots through the

00:07:48.439 --> 00:07:52.619
early phase of their careers flying small
jets for low pay and get more pilots flying

00:07:52.619 --> 00:07:54.599
the larger planes earlier.

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:58.879
That leaves the brunt of the impact of this
shortage on small towns served by these small

00:07:58.879 --> 00:07:59.879
planes.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:04.429
US airlines especially, thanks to the country’s
vastness, operate with route networks emphasizing

00:08:04.429 --> 00:08:06.309
service to small airports.

00:08:06.309 --> 00:08:11.819
Rather amazingly, there are 553 airports in
the US with commercial service.

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:16.339
United is perhaps the most focused among the
big three US airlines on small town service

00:08:16.339 --> 00:08:21.869
with 235 domestic destinations but, due to
the lack of pilots, it’s having to cancel

00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:22.899
routes.

00:08:22.899 --> 00:08:27.610
In September 2018 alone the airline stopped
service from Chicago to Willard, Illinois,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.250
Mobile, Alabama, and Manchester, New Hampshire
even though these routes were likely profitable.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.680
United isn’t alone in this.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.160
Horizon Air, the subsidiary operating Alaska
Airlines’ regional flights, had to cancel

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:42.720
thousands of flights in 2017 due to a severe
lack of pilots.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.050
There are a couple of possible solutions to
this crisis.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:50.710
The most obvious one is to increase pay and
to improve working conditions but, from the

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:55.060
airlines’ perspectives, that’s the last-ditch
solution as they focus on their bottom lines.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:58.820
While it’s tough to sympathize with these
billion dollar businesses losing some money

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.670
as a result of paying a more livable wage,
the reality is that if they’re having to

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:07.500
pay more for the pilots to serve small towns
it’s going to cost more to fly to small

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.770
towns and those living in small towns are
often the ones least able to pay.

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:15.740
Another commonly proposed solution is to attract
more women pilots.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:20.600
Only 6.7% of the world’s pilots are female
and so, by putting more effort into recruiting

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.600
that half of the population airlines could
potentially increase pilot numbers without

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.260
increasing pay.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.862
More and more airlines, such as Lufthansa
and Emirates, are also operating up their

00:09:29.862 --> 00:09:33.870
own flights schools where cadets can train
for reduced rates or for free as long as they

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.160
end up working at the airline.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.220
Airlines are also working to reduce the number
of pilots needed.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:43.540
Emirates, for example, reduced the number
of pilots it uses on some of its longest flights.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:48.660
On the 13 hour flight from Dubai to Sydney,
for example, they now only staff three pilots

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.890
instead of four which means that each only
gets four hours of inflight rest instead of

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:53.890
six.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:58.080
Finally, one of the more controversial proposals
to fix this issue has been to increase automation

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.110
in the cockpit to the point where only one
or possibly zero pilots are needed to fly.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:07.660
This proposal has met criticism from pilots,
cabin crew, and the public alike as many opponents

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:11.230
point out that the cockpit is already highly
automated and the pilot’s main role is to

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:13.340
troubleshoot if things go wrong.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.920
Nonetheless, with captains of wide-body planes
earning more than $300 per flight hour the

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.790
cost of crewing the cockpit can be as much
as $19,000 each way on the longest flights

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:26.750
like San Francisco to Singapore so there’s
certainly financial pressure on airlines to

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:29.340
cut down the need for pilots if they can.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:33.310
There are thousands of people who become pilots
each year but there are millions more who

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:34.790
want to become pilots.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:40.020
To bring more people into the industry all
airlines need to do is to lower the extraordinarily

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:45.060
high barrier to entry since as the industry
grows worldwide, the allure of the skies won’t

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.910
be enough to get enough pilots into the cockpit.

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:52.750
If you want to become a pilot two of the major
things you absolutely need to have a solid

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.230
grasp of are math and physics.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.610
Brilliant is a great place to learn those
two subjects since their courses break down

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:02.490
concepts into their intuitive principles and
build them back up so you don’t just memorize

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:04.670
how do something, you understand it.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:09.380
They also have plenty of other courses on
super interesting subjects like logic, probability,

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:11.550
astronomy, machine learning, and more.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.950
They really put a lot of work into perfecting
each and every course they have and best of

